       "whyThisIsARegistryLaw": "`fact.anchors` is `minItems`-free in the identity schema and only `clones` ever stated a cardinality. Every predicate written over the array - the `file` row's `anchorPathField`, and native-evidence section 1.2's `every one of its anchor paths` - is universally quantified, so at ZERO anchors it is vacuously TRUE and neither permits nor forbids that spelling. On the frozen v13 reference BOTH the whole-file-anchored and the unanchored spelling of one `file@enumerated` claim close a Run, with DIFFERENT fact2 identities (`5cce76d9...` and `d7bb2235...` over `tool/main.py`). A universally quantified predicate over an array of unstated length is not a closed law; the cardinality has to be stated.",
       "classes": {
         "source-text": {
-          "cardinality": "minimum 1",
+          "cardinality": "minimum 1, no additional relation-specific maximum",
           "members": [
             "calls",
             "control-flow",
@@ -31,7 +31,7 @@
             "unresolved-edge"
           ],
           "rule": "A code fact is READ FROM SOURCE TEXT, so it must name at least one anchor: the span it was read from. An unanchored code fact names no file that could have been read and is refused (`FACT_ANCHOR_CARDINALITY`). This was previously enforced only inside the syntax-universe capability guard, so the same unanchored `declares` or `references` fact closed a Run under a TypeScript or Rust universe; the law is one law and now binds in every universe.",
-          "noIdentityEqualityPromise": "There is deliberately NO upper bound and NO canonical extent. Which spans a producer cites for a multi-span claim is a genuine semantic choice about what was read, so two conformant producers that cite different spans have produced DIFFERENT semantic descriptors and legitimately mint different fact2 identities. Nothing here promises cross-provider anchor identity for code facts, and asserting one would be a false equality between two different pieces of evidence."
+          "noIdentityEqualityPromise": "There is deliberately NO ADDITIONAL relation-specific maximum and no canonical extent BEYOND THE COMMON SCHEMA BOUNDS - foundation fact.anchors already carries maxItems 100000 and uniqueItems, and those continue to apply to every relation. An earlier revision said there is no upper bound at all, which contradicted that global bound; what this class declines to impose is a per-relation ceiling or a canonical span set, not the shared one. Which spans a producer cites for a multi-span claim is a genuine semantic choice about what was read, so two conformant producers that cite different spans have produced DIFFERENT semantic descriptors and legitimately mint different fact2 identities. Nothing here promises cross-provider anchor identity for code facts, and asserting one would be a false equality between two different pieces of evidence."
         },
         "body-identity": {
           "cardinality": "exactly 1",
@@ -49,7 +49,9 @@
           ],
           "rule": "An inventory fact is NOT read from a source span. Its whole semantic input is its payload, and that payload is already bound to the snapshot by this relation's own `snapshotJoins`: the path must be in the snapshot inventory, and for `file` the content digest, the byte length and CUSTODY of the exact retained bytes are joined as well. An anchor could only restate a subset of a claim that is already joined, and because nothing derives it, it is a free axis that gives one semantic input several spellings and several identities. It is therefore closed at zero (`FACT_ANCHOR_CARDINALITY`).",
           "whatThisMakesRepresentable": "Exactly zero is the only cardinality every inventoried path can satisfy, which is what makes the unconditional inventory promise honest: a path no bundled grammar reads (`tool/main.py`), an extensionless path (`LICENSE`), a data-document path, and - decisively - a `vcs-change` whose `changeKind` is `deleted`, whose path is by construction NOT in the analysed snapshot and can therefore carry no anchor at all. A cardinality of one would make that last case unrepresentable and would newly require the manifest bytes of every `package` fact to be retained, neither of which any contract asks for.",
-          "whatItAlsoCloses": "The `file` row's `anchorPathField` was the only defence against an arbitrary anchor borrowed to satisfy an inventory claim, and it covered `file` alone: on frozen v13 a `package@manifest-declared` fact claiming `package.json` closed a Run while anchored into an unrelated `a.ts`. Closing the class at zero refuses that for all three inventory relations with one reason. `anchorPathField` is retained as the narrower per-row statement it always was; under this cardinality it can no longer be reached for `file`, and that is the point - the broader law subsumes it rather than leaving a predicate whose only remaining reading is the vacuous one."
+          "whatItAlsoCloses": "The `file` row's `anchorPathField` was the only defence against an arbitrary anchor borrowed to satisfy an inventory claim, and it covered `file` alone: on frozen v13 a `package@manifest-declared` fact claiming `package.json` closed a Run while anchored into an unrelated `a.ts`. Closing the class at zero refuses that for all three inventory relations with one reason. `anchorPathField` is retained as the narrower per-row statement it always was; under this cardinality it can no longer be reached for `file`, and that is the point - the broader law subsumes it rather than leaving a predicate whose only remaining reading is the vacuous one.",
+          "rawByteBoundary": "An inventory claim retains path, content digest and byte length WITHOUT anyone decoding the bytes. At zero anchors the ANCHOR_SOURCE/ANCHOR_RANGE/ANCHOR_UTF8 checks never run for such a fact - they iterate the anchors - while the inventoried-file join still does, and it only hashes and measures. An EMPTY file and a NON-UTF8 BINARY file are therefore ordinary inventory, at an extensionless path as readily as any other; requiring bytes to parse as code or as UTF8 merely to record their path and hash would narrow the promise. The source-text and body-identity classes keep exactly the scope they had: the SAME non-UTF8 bytes still refuse as a code-span anchor (ANCHOR_UTF8), and the digest, length and path joins still refuse a wrong claim about those bytes.",
+          "locationRouteAfterRemovingAnchors": "Removing inventory anchors removes NO location route, and none is invented to replace them. Nothing reads fact.anchors as a finding location: a finding locates through finding-fingerprint.subjectKey.logicalPath, an inventory claim's own location is its payload path joined to the snapshot inventory by this relation's snapshotJoins, and finding, finding-fingerprint, proof-bundle and predicate-witness carry no anchor field at all. A finding over a zero-anchor inventory fact closes a Run and is located by exactly those existing records."
         }
       },
       "notAGrammarQuestion": "Anchor cardinality and syntax-universe capability are different laws and neither substitutes for the other. The grammar-capability registry decides which relation@rung a SELECTED grammar can bear; this law decides how many spans a fact of that relation cites. Inventory capabilities are ungated by grammar at BOTH enforcement boundaries (see the native grammar-capability registry `enforcementBoundaries`), and independently carry no anchors at all.",
@@ -211,6 +213,15 @@
         "coverageTotality": {
           "rung": "enumerated",
           "over": "subjects",
+          "matchOn": [
+            "snapshotId",
+            "relation",
+            "resolution",
+            "sourceUniverse",
