+          "matchLaw": "A fact discharges THIS scope's obligation only if it agrees with the scope on EVERY coordinate listed in matchOn. Those are the four CoverageKeyV2 coordinates other than the subject-scope commitment itself, plus the owning snapshot. An earlier revision matched relation and resolution ALONE, and that is not enough in a view that carries more than one universe: the view-level fact/scope join is EXISTENTIAL - each fact must agree with SOME scope on relation, rung and both universes - so a TypeScript file fact is lawfully in a view that also carries a syntax-universe scope, and it then paid that scope's missing-file obligation. A complete Run exhibited exactly that (root diagnostic attempt3: two complete file@enumerated scopes for `a.ts`, only the TypeScript fact, ADMIT). Totality is a claim about what THIS universe examined, so it must be discharged by evidence produced in THIS universe; a fact from another universe is a different interpretation of the same bytes and is not the same observation.",
+          "snapshotIsListedDeliberately": "The owning snapshot is already forced transitively - Run closure requires every fact and every subject-scope to carry the Run's own snapshotId - so listing it changes no outcome today, and a control confirms the transitive refusal (REFERENCE_SOURCE_JOIN). It is listed anyway because this law reads ONE scope's obligation out of a SHARED view, and a join that silently depends on an invariant enforced somewhere else is the same class of implicit coupling that produced the cross-universe defect.",
           "pathField": "path",
